---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Kbd from "../components/Kbd.astro";

const title = "Why viewbus — what's actually going on under the hood";
const description =
  "The design decisions behind viewbus: a local SQLite index, non-destructive AMQP peeks, bulk operations that survive throttling, and cheap background monitoring.";
const path = "/why-viewbus";

const breadcrumbSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
  itemListElement: [
    { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: "https://viewbus.app/" },
    { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Why viewbus", item: "https://viewbus.app/why-viewbus/" },
  ],
};

const techArticleSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "TechArticle",
  headline: "Why viewbus — the design decisions behind the app",
  description,
  url: "https://viewbus.app/why-viewbus/",
  author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Håkon Lia" },
};

// Short, checkable claims. Each one is something a sceptical reader could
// verify by watching the app, not marketing adjectives.
const receipts = [
  ["Local index", "SQLite on your machine, not a live call per click"],
  ["Peeks are non-destructive", "no dequeue, no delivery-count change"],
  ["Secrets in the OS keychain", "never in the index, never in logs"],
  ["No telemetry", "four outbound hosts, all of them yours or Microsoft's"],
];
---
<Base title={title} description={description} path={path}>
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <script type="application/ld+json" is:inline set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbSchema)} />
    <script type="application/ld+json" is:inline set:html={JSON.stringify(techArticleSchema)} />
  </Fragment>

  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 py-16 md:py-20">
    <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold tracking-tight leading-[1.05]">
      Why viewbus
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-5 text-lg text-[var(--color-muted)] leading-relaxed">
      Most Service Bus tooling makes you wait — for a portal blade to load, for a tree to expand, for
      a peek to come back. viewbus is built around the opposite assumption: that you already know what
      you're looking for, and the tool's job is to get out of the way.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-muted)] leading-relaxed">
      Here's what that actually means in the code, rather than on a feature list.
    </p>

    <ul class="mt-8 grid gap-x-8 gap-y-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
      {receipts.map(([term, def]) => (
        <li class="flex gap-2.5">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="17" height="17" fill="none" stroke="var(--color-success)" stroke-width="2.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="mt-0.5 shrink-0" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 6 9 17l-5-5" /></svg>
          <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed">
            <span class="font-medium text-[var(--color-fg)]">{term}</span>
            <span class="text-[var(--color-muted)]"> — {def}</span>
          </p>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>

    <section class="mt-16">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">It reads an index, not the cloud</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-muted)] leading-relaxed">
        The first time you sign in, viewbus walks your subscriptions and writes every namespace, queue,
        topic, and subscription into a local SQLite database. After that, browsing the tree is a disk
        read. Expanding a namespace on a tenant with thousands of resources is instant, because nothing
        is being fetched — the answer is already on your machine.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-muted)] leading-relaxed">
        The index refreshes when your attention lands somewhere: you click a namespace, you jump to a
        resource from spotlight, you run an operation, or you come back to the window after a while.
        There's no background polling loop burning API quota while you're in a meeting, and no
        five-minute-old cache silently lying to you about a queue you're actively looking at.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-muted)] leading-relaxed">
        That's also what makes search worth using. Fuzzy matching runs in Rust against the local
        index, so it returns as fast as you type — across every resource, not just the branch you
        happen to have open.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-14">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">It speaks AMQP, and it respects the broker</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-muted)] leading-relaxed">
        Reading messages is where a Service Bus tool can quietly do damage, so this is the part worth
        being precise about. viewbus browses messages over AMQP using peek semantics: messages are read
        without being received. Nothing is dequeued, no delivery count is incremented, and no message
        gets one step closer to the dead-letter queue because you looked at it. On session-enabled
        entities it takes a brief session lock while reading and releases it — the same thing the SDK
        would do, done deliberately.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-muted)] leading-relaxed">
        Because peeking is safe, the interface can be generous with it. You can page through a
        dead-letter queue, filter by session, message ID, correlation ID, subject, or dead-letter
        reason, and search inside message bodies — across every queue, topic, and DLQ in a namespace at
        once. That last one is the thing neither the classic Service Bus Explorer nor the Azure Portal
        can do, and it's the difference between finding a stuck order in ten seconds and opening
        fourteen resources by hand.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-muted)] leading-relaxed">
        Namespaces you reach by connection string work the same way. They're discovered through the
        Service Bus admin API with a SAS token, indexed into the same SQLite tables as
        Azure-discovered ones, and every downstream feature — peek, resend, spotlight, MCP — treats
        them identically. The only difference you'll notice is where they appear in the sidebar.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-14">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Fixing a message is an edit, not a rewrite</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-muted)] leading-relaxed">
        When something dead-letters because of one bad field, the fix shouldn't involve reconstructing
        the message by hand. Open it, edit the body and properties in a side-by-side diff editor, and
        resend — you see exactly what changed before anything goes back on the bus. You can also
        compose new messages outright, with full control over properties, content type, and scheduling.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-14">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Bulk operations that survive contact with reality</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-muted)] leading-relaxed">
        Draining a dead-letter queue of forty thousand messages is not the same problem as draining one
        of forty. Service Bus will throttle you, connections drop, and a naive loop either dies halfway
        or hammers the namespace until it does.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-muted)] leading-relaxed">
        So bulk work in viewbus runs as a job. You preview the batch the filter matches before anything
        happens, then watch it run with live progress and an ETA, and you can cancel mid-flight.
        Throttling responses are handled with backoff rather than treated as failure. Every run —
        purge, move-to-dead-letter, bulk resend, bulk delete — is recorded in a per-resource history,
        so "what happened to those messages" has an answer.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-14">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Monitoring you can leave running</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-muted)] leading-relaxed">
        Dead-letter counts get sampled in the background on a cadence you choose. You group the queues,
        topics, and subscriptions you care about into named sets, give each set a threshold — "tell me
        if any production DLQ goes over 25" — and get a native notification the moment one trips.
        Anything that turns out to be noise can be muted for an hour or a day; the mute expires by
        itself, and if the problem is still there, you hear about it again.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-muted)] leading-relaxed">
        It's deliberately cheap. Sampling counts is a metadata call, not a message read, and it runs
        from the app you already have open rather than from infrastructure you'd have to pay for.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-14">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Spotlight, because tree-clicking is slow</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-muted)] leading-relaxed">
        <Kbd>Ctrl</Kbd> <Kbd>I</Kbd> anywhere on your machine opens a fuzzy search over every indexed
        resource. Type three characters of a queue name, hit Enter, and viewbus comes to the front on
        that resource. It works when the app is closed to the tray, so it behaves like an OS-level
        launcher for your Service Bus topology rather than a feature you have to go find. The hotkey is
        rebindable, and the rest of the app is keyboard-driven too.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-14">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Two ways to automate it</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-muted)] leading-relaxed">
        A <a href="/mcp-server/" class="text-[var(--color-accent)] hover:underline">Model Context Protocol server</a>
        ships inside the app, so Claude, Cursor, or Codex can answer questions like "how many dead
        letters piled up on the orders topic between 11 and 3, and what do they have in common". It's
        read-only against your bus, holds no credentials of its own, and applies filters inside viewbus
        so message bytes don't get dumped into a model's context unless you ask for a specific body.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-muted)] leading-relaxed">
        For everything that isn't an AI, there's a
        <a href="/developers/" class="text-[var(--color-accent)] hover:underline">local HTTP API</a> on
        <code class="ic">127.0.0.1</code>: check status, search the index, focus a resource. It's what
        you'd wire a Raycast or PowerToys Run command into, or a VS Code task, or a shell script. Both
        surfaces are local-only — no listener on your network, nothing routed through a server of ours.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-14">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Nothing about you leaves</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-muted)] leading-relaxed">
        There's no backend, so there's nothing to trust us with. Sign-in is an OAuth flow straight to
        Microsoft; tokens and connection strings live in the Windows Credential Manager or the macOS
        Keychain, never in the index and never in a log file. The app's content security policy allows
        exactly four destinations: Microsoft's login and management endpoints, your own
        <code class="ic">*.servicebus.windows.net</code> namespaces, and two static JSON files on this
        domain for update and announcement checks. Open the network inspector and count them.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-muted)] leading-relaxed">
        The full breakdown — every file written, every host contacted — is on the
        <a href="/privacy/" class="text-[var(--color-accent)] hover:underline">privacy page</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-14">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">It's a native app, and it's free</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-muted)] leading-relaxed">
        viewbus is Rust and a system webview via Tauri, not a bundled browser. It starts fast, the
        installer is small, and the heavy lifting — indexing, fuzzy search, AMQP, bulk jobs — happens in
        compiled code rather than in JavaScript. It runs natively on Windows and on Apple Silicon.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-muted)] leading-relaxed">
        There's no paid tier, no seat count, and no account. It's a tool one developer wanted to exist;
        if it saves you an afternoon,
        <a href="https://buymeacoffee.com/hakonlia" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-[var(--color-accent)] hover:underline">a coffee</a>
        is welcome and never expected.
      </p>
    </section>

    <div class="mt-16 flex flex-col gap-3 border-t border-[var(--color-border)] pt-8 sm:flex-row sm:items-center">
      <a
        href="/download/"
        class="inline-flex items-center justify-center rounded-full h-11 px-6 text-sm font-medium bg-[var(--color-fg)] text-[var(--color-bg)] transition-colors hover:bg-[color-mix(in_oklab,var(--color-fg)_88%,transparent)]"
      >Download viewbus</a>
      <a
        href="/service-bus-explorer-alternative/"
        class="inline-flex items-center justify-center rounded-full h-11 px-6 text-sm font-medium border border-[var(--color-border)] transition-colors hover:bg-[var(--color-hover)]"
      >Coming from Service Bus Explorer?</a>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>
